    const response = await fetch('https://example.com/data');
    const data = await response.json();
    updateUI(data);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
  }
}

function updateUI(data) {
  // Update the UI with the retrieved data
  console.log(data);
}

fetchDataAndUpdateUI();
```
In this example, we define an `async` function `fetchDataAndUpdateUI` that makes a network request to retrieve some data using the `fetch` function. We use the `await` keyword to pause the execution of the function until the Promise is resolved. Once the data is retrieved, we update the UI with the retrieved data using the `updateUI` function. We also use a `try/catch` block to handle any errors that may occur during the execution of the function.

**COMMON MISTAKE:**

One common mistake that learners make when using `async/await` syntax is forgetting to handle errors properly. When using `async/await` syntax, it is essential to use a `try/catch` block to catch any errors that may occur during the execution of the function. If an error occurs and is not caught, the function will throw an error and terminate abruptly.

